# Stormhook — released 2026-09-05 (Saturday, Pacific)

**This was a forced two-week-clock release, not a quality-based one.** The
routine's rule fired: Pacific Saturday, and the game started 2026-08-27, which
is 9 days. Under a forced release the bar is *not broken*, not *finished* —
cosmetic shortfalls ship as debt and are listed below. Read this whole file
before assuming anything about Stormhook's quality.

Released as the featured game of the Bot Built Arcade and promoted to `main`.

---

## What release night actually verified

Every one of these was run tonight against the shipped commit, not quoted from
an earlier log:

* **Tests: 13 suites / 329 tests green** (`npx jest`). Includes 37 rope/physics
  simulation tests, 26 design invariants, and the autopilot wedge regression.
* **Real-browser campaign smoke** (`tools/smoke.js --levels 5`, headless
  Chromium): boots to `playing`, a live 510 px/s input-driven swing, live rope
  wrapping onto a pylon (3 pivots), **all five levels clear** (final score
  32,978), the run ends in `gameover`, the storm kills and the level restarts
  cleanly, no horizontal scrollbar, console clean.
* **Six-viewport screenshot sweep** at 320x568, 390x844, 844x390, 768x1024,
  1440x900 and 3840x2160. Every capture stayed in `playing`, matched its
  requested dimensions, reported `hScroll:false`, and had an empty app console.
  **The PNGs were opened and read**, plus a pixel-crop of the landscape HUD
  corner and both title screens.
* **One glaring defect was found by that sweep and fixed** (`8acf2bc`): the
  `STORMHOOK` wordmark measured 379px inside a 356px content box, so the final
  K hung over the briefing card's right border on every viewport >= 768px —
  on the first screen a desktop player sees. Now 346px, measured inside the box
  at all six viewports. The same commit stopped `3 · RELEASE` splitting across
  two lines on touch phones.
* The only console error in any run is a proxy-blocked request to
  `googletagmanager.com`. That is the arcade's site-wide analytics tag, present
  on all twelve game pages and the root `index.html`. It is human-added and
  deliberate; it is **not** a SPEC violation and must not be "fixed".

No crash, no console errors, no unreadable HUD, no broken touch control, no
uncompletable level. That is the forced-release bar, and it is met.

---

## Final critic verdicts — honest, and two of them are FAILs

**No discipline ever recorded a passing blind side-by-side verdict, and no
independent shipping judge was ever run.** Stormhook would not have been
eligible for a quality-based release. It shipped on the calendar.

| Discipline | File | Final verdict |
|---|---|---|
| Renderer | `render.js` | **FAIL** vs *Ori and the Blind Forest* / *Rayman Legends* |
| UI | `ui.js`, `game.css` | **FAIL** vs *Umihara Kawase Fresh!* / *Bionic Commando Rearmed* |
| Audio | `audio.js` | **UNJUDGED** — rewritten, never faced a critic |
| VFX | `particles.js` | **UNJUDGED** — no critic loop was ever run |
| Textures | `textures.js` | **UNJUDGED** — no critic loop was ever run |

* **Renderer — FAIL.** The critic granted that the salvager silhouette, hull
  variation and motion cues are materially improved and no longer read as
  scaffold. It still held that Stormhook loses on route-focused composition,
  distinct depth planes, surface-reactive lighting, authored structures and
  environmental storytelling; that portrait divides into long atmospheric
  bands; and that desktop has a low-information centre. Later nights added
  derived salvage rigs, a world-anchored beacon shaft and route wrecks dropped
  into the swing lane — real improvements, but they were never re-judged, so
  the standing verdict remains FAIL.
* **UI — FAIL.** The critic granted that the HUD leak is fixed, `×1` is
  immediately readable, the player/tether relationship is clearer and the
  contextual touch onboarding is a real improvement. It still held that the
  title is an oversized web-style card in an empty scene, the coach is detached
  from its target, the bottom controls form competing zones, and the playfield
  hierarchy is weaker than the commercial references. The final review after
  `90ee8bf` came back **UNJUDGED** because no renderable *Bionic Commando*
  reference could be fetched, and noted the title salvager is still too subtle
  for an AAA first-action scene.

---

## Polish debt carried into release

1. **Renderer composition — the largest single loss.** No depth occlusion, no
   material specificity, thin environmental storytelling, no surface-reactive
   lighting. The route still reads as a horizontal strip rather than an
   authored place. This is where the next attempt at this game should start.
2. **Title screen presentation.** Still a large card over a quiet scene rather
   than a composed first-action shot; the hook-line motif reads as a diagram.
   The wordmark now fits, but "fits" is not "designed".
3. **Audio is entirely unjudged.** A substantial `audio.js` rewrite landed on
   2026-09-01 as a checkpoint commit and no critic has ever heard it. It is not
   known to be bad; it is known to be unverified.
4. **VFX and textures never had a critic loop at all.** They are lead-authored
   first-pass scaffolds that were never handed to their owning agents.
5. **Real-GPU performance is completely unverified.** Every fps figure in this
   game's history comes from software rasterisation in a GPU-less sandbox and
   is not comparable across sessions — tonight's sweep read 38/22/22/11/23/5
   across the six viewports in order. 4K in particular is an unquantified
   profiling risk. Nothing has ever run on real hardware.
6. **The autopilot clears only level 1.** `tools/autopilot.js` crosses *Shallow
   Wrecks* end to end but dies to a deck hazard at 41%/31%/16% on levels 2-4
   and falls at 11% on level 5. Levels 2-5 are therefore machine-playable only
   in the weaker sense that the design invariants prove anchor coverage and the
   smoke test clears them by teleport-assisted progression. **Do not weaken a
   level to raise those numbers — improve the bot.**
7. **HUD scale at 4K.** The HUD is laid out in CSS pixels and is proportionally
   very small on a 3840x2160 display. Legible, but not designed for that size.

## Landmines for whoever touches this game next

* **A green Jest suite is not evidence the game runs.** The vm harness loads
  only `core.js`, `levels.js` and `physics.js`. On 2026-09-01 a
  `ReferenceError: TILE is not defined` in `render.js` broke the game in every
  browser for a full day while 308 tests stayed green. Any change to
  `render.js`, `ui.js`, `particles.js` or `textures.js` must run
  `tools/smoke.js` before it is pushed.
* **The browser harness works in this sandbox.** Several runs wrongly reported
  it unavailable and fell back to code review, which is how the above bug
  survived. `TESTING.md` carries the exact recipe; the short version is that
  Chromium is at `/opt/pw-browsers` and only the npm driver is missing, and
  `SH_CHROME` is mandatory because the driver pins a different browser build.
